 ## Efficiency
 
-Count calls as productive when they materially discover, implement, verify, or
-recover the requested result. Count calls as wasted when they repeat known work,
-read unrelated bulk data, retry the same failure without a changed approach, or
-continue after the answer is established.
-
-Do not penalize a short session merely for being short. One well-grounded call
-can be efficient and high quality. Do not penalize necessary recovery caused by
-an external provider or environment failure as if it were agent waste.
+Before judging efficiency, reconstruct the material call/result sequence using
+session and raw-line order. For each visible failed call or bulk search, check
+what the worker already knew, what the matching result actually returned, and
+what later work used it. Cite this sequence in the finding. A source read before
+a broad search cannot be treated as a discovery made by that search.
+
+Read error flags with returned text: partial output and failure can coexist.
+When a cause or duration is clipped, state that it is unknown; descriptions such
+as cheap, quick, or harmless also need evidence. A useful final result establishes
+delivery, not the productivity of every preceding call. Keep avoidable work
+visible alongside successful delivery, with its simpler available alternative.
+Necessary exploration and proportionate recovery can be useful even when a call
+fails. Call count, a failure, or a short session alone does not establish waste.
 
